**Describe the bug**
The JSON parser returns when an unexpected nested object is closed with `}`, instead of continuing to parse the rest of the JSON.

**To Reproduce**
I have created a PR with tests (and a fix) that shows the behavior:
https://github.com/zephyrproject-rtos/zephyr/pull/55757

This works:
```C
ZTEST(lib_json_test, test_json_key_not_in_descr_partial)
{
	struct test_struct ts;
	char encoded[] = "{\"key_not_in_descr\":123456, \"some_int\": 123}";
	int ret;

	ret = json_obj_parse(encoded, sizeof(encoded) - 1, test_descr,
			     ARRAY_SIZE(test_descr), &ts);
	zassert_equal(ret, 0b10, "some_int should be decoded");
}
```

This does not:
```C
ZTEST(lib_json_test, test_json_key_obj_not_in_descr_partial)
{
	struct test_struct ts;
	char encoded[] = "{\"key_not_in_descr\":{}, \"some_int\": 123}";
	int ret;

	ret = json_obj_parse(encoded, sizeof(encoded) - 1, test_descr,
			     ARRAY_SIZE(test_descr), &ts);
	zassert_equal(ret, 0b10, "some_int should be decoded");
}
```

**Expected behavior**
Since the JSON parser does not barf on unrecognized keys that are strings or numbers, I would expect it to continue parsing also in this case. I personally expect the parser to ignore all keys that it doesn't know, but I would like to clarify if that is the library's design goal.

**Impact**
I can design around this in my application but any JSON-accepting application could be stopped from working by inserting a stray object at the start of the JSON data.

**Logs and console output**
See GH Action for my PR:
```
START - test_json_key_not_in_descr_partial
 PASS - test_json_key_not_in_descr_partial in 0.001 seconds
===================================================================
START - test_json_key_obj_not_in_descr_partial
    Assertion failed at WEST_TOPDIR/zephyr/tests/lib/json/src/main.c:579: lib_json_test_test_json_key_obj_not_in_descr_partial: (ret not equal to 0b10)
some_int should be decoded
 FAIL - test_json_key_obj_not_in_descr_partial in 0.002 seconds
```

**Environment (please complete the following information):**
See PR linked above.

review_comments:
I'd suggest to check also if the decoded value is correct. Same for test below.

```suggestion
	zassert_equal(ret, 0b10, "some_int should be decoded");
	zassert_equal(ts.some_int, 123, "wrong value of some_int");
```
